            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array filled with zeros, in the shape you give it.\n\nOn its own it is the dullest array there is, and that is its use: a blank canvas. Start from zeros and write into it with np.edit, add frames into it to accumulate, or hold a correctly shaped place in a chain until real data arrives.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.zeros <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nOne number per dimension: np.zeros 8 8 is an 8 by 8 array, np.zeros 16 a\nrow of 16. With no arguments you get a 1-element array. The NUMBER of\ndimensions is fixed when the node is made - retype it to change that.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.zeros 8 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nClick it, or send it anything, to send the array. The node sends nothing until it is clicked; each click sends a fresh copy, so whatever is done to one copy downstream never reaches the next.\n\ndim 0, dim 1, ... (properties):\nThe size of each dimension, used on the next click.\n\ndtype (option):\nfloat32 (the default), float, double, int64, uint8 or bool (all false).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray of zeros:\nThe array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe load_bang clicks np.zeros once when the page opens. The left heat_map is the blank array - every cell 0, one flat colour. np.edit then writes 1 into rows 2 to 5 and columns 1 to 6 (indices 2:6, 1:7 - the end of a range is not included), and the right heat_map shows the result. Type other indices or another value (0 to 1) into np.edit to draw something else on the same blank.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.ones is the same with every value 1. t.zeros is the PyTorch version. np.edit writes values into part of an array; np.rand fills one with noise instead.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 640,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array filled with zeros, in the shape you give it.\n\nOn its own it is the dullest array there is, and that is its use: a blank canvas. Start from zeros and write into it with np.edit, add frames into it to accumulate, or hold a correctly shaped place in a chain until real data arrives.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.zeros <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nOne number per dimension: np.zeros 8 8 is an 8 by 8 array, np.zeros 16 a\nrow of 16. With no arguments you get a 1-element array. The NUMBER of\ndimensions is fixed when the node is made - retype it to change that.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.zeros 8 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nClick it, or send it anything, to send the array. The node sends nothing until it is clicked; each click sends a fresh copy, so whatever is done to one copy downstream never reaches the next.\n\ndim 0, dim 1, ... (properties):\nThe size of each dimension, used on the next click.\n\ndtype (option):\nfloat32 (the default), float, double, int64, uint8 or bool (all false).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray of zeros:\nThe array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe load_bang clicks np.zeros once when the page opens. The left heat_map is the blank array - every cell 0, one flat colour. np.edit then writes 1 into rows 2 to 5 and columns 1 to 6 (indices 2:6, 1:7 - the end of a range is not included), and the right heat_map shows the result. Type other indices or another value (0 to 1) into np.edit to draw something else on the same blank.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.ones is the same with every value 1. t.zeros is the PyTorch version. np.edit writes values into part of an array; np.rand fills one with noise instead."
        },
